WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
Chapter 1 showed a motor walking by gripping, swinging, releasing, and resetting.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:11.420
But how can experiments reveal the direction, steps, and pull of a machine too small

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.420
to resolve?

4
00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:17.247
Begin with a gliding assay.

5
00:00:18.707 --> 00:00:22.627
Purified motors are fastened by their tails to a glass coverslip.

6
00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:25.547
Add fluorescent tracks and ATP.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.987
The motors cannot travel, but their feet can still walk.

8
00:00:31.247 --> 00:00:36.227
A fluorescence microscope follows the long glowing tracks; each motor is far too

9
00:00:36.227 --> 00:00:37.227
small to resolve.

10
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:41.617
Imagine a free motor that walks right on a fixed track.

11
00:00:42.997 --> 00:00:46.777
Clamp that motor instead, and every stroke pushes the loose track left.

12
00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:50.857
The same interaction now moves the other partner.

13
00:00:51.577 --> 00:00:55.517
It resembles walking on a loose rug while bolted in place.

14
00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:59.690
Mark the track's two ends, then watch which end leads.

15
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:05.590
Because the track glides opposite the motor's walk, that movie reveals direction.

16
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.770
It also reports speed across many motors at once.

17
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.940
To see one motor, reverse the geometry again.

18
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.300
Fix the blue track.

19
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.740
Attach one motor to a yellow bead, then place that bead inside a focused laser trap.

20
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.780
Sparse coating makes most beads carry at most one motor, separating one walker from

21
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.780
the crowd.

22
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:34.663
Suppose the motor advances in equal jumps, with waits between them.

23
00:01:35.843 --> 00:01:38.143
What shape should the bead's position record make?

24
00:01:39.383 --> 00:01:40.383
Pause and predict.

25
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.940
A staircase. Flat stretches are waits; sudden rises are steps.

26
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.600
Kinesin bead records showed rises 8 nanometres high.

27
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.080
Timing the flat stretches shows how long the motor waits before each jump.

28
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.000
The invisible walk became a measured step size and a step rate.

29
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.270
The trap also behaves like a spring.

30
00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.590
Pull the bead from the centre, and the restoring force grows.

31
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.690
After calibration, displacement becomes force.

32
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.910
Trap stiffness says how much force each nanometre of displacement adds.

33
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.070
Farther from centre means a larger opposing load.

34
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:33.633
Eventually the motor may stall: it keeps trying, but makes no net progress.

35
00:02:35.013 --> 00:02:37.733
The bead's offset then reports the opposing force.

36
00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:43.393
Different loads reveal how the motor's speed changes as resistance grows.

37
00:02:43.393 --> 00:02:49.233
Position, time, and calibration turn motion into steps, speed, and pull.

38
00:02:50.317 --> 00:02:52.317
But a number belongs to its setup.

39
00:02:53.597 --> 00:02:56.557
In a fixed trap, load rises as the bead moves.

40
00:02:58.217 --> 00:02:59.997
Feedback can hold load constant instead.

41
00:03:01.597 --> 00:03:04.877
Compare motors only under matched fuel and loading conditions.

42
00:03:06.137 --> 00:03:10.957
Otherwise, a neat calculation can combine measurements that never occurred together.

43
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.730
One bench, two choices.

44
00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.570
Fix the motor, and the track reports direction.

45
00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.810
Hold the bead, and its motion reports steps and force.

46
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.230
Change what is held still, and motion becomes a measurement.
